#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""oma-runner: runs Oma's builders on the Mac Pro for the agent on the Fold. See docs/MAC-RUNNER-SPEC.md.

It listens on the Mac's Tailscale address only, and every request must carry a key this runner gave to the very device
asking: each device Boss pairs has a key of its own, and `tailscale whois` must name that device as the caller, so a key
copied anywhere else opens nothing. The Mac keeps only each key's SHA-256, in ~/agents/claude-code/oma-runner.keys.json,
so an agent that reads that file cannot use it. It does one thing: run a coding agent (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor or Grok) in a
named project thread, or talk with a Venice model, under a folder Boss allowed, and stream what it says. It is not a shell: there is no endpoint that runs a command (a bot's shell tool runs one only as a tool call from that bot's own turn, and only a few look-only commands without a card). It
approves nothing itself: every request for approval waits for the phone's answer, however long that takes.

  GET  /health                           {ok, working, waiting, kinds, models: {venice: [...]}}
  GET  /threads                          every thread: id, name, summary, status, last_worked, tail, pending, bot
  GET  /bots/ID                          one bot this machine hosts: its settings, instructions and notes (read only)
  POST /threads/ID/fresh                 a bot's session starts over: its conversation is archived, its notes stay
  POST /threads   {name, project, kind, model, mode}   makes one under an allowed root; kind claude (default), codex, cursor, grok or venice;
                                         mode auto (default) or manual
  GET  /threads/ID                       one thread
  POST /threads/ID/messages   {text}     the owner's words; {taken: "now"} or {taken: "queued", position}
  GET  /threads/ID/stream[?after=N]      server-sent events from event N+1 on (or the Last-Event-ID header)
  GET  /threads/ID/events[?after=N]      the same events as plain JSON, for asking without holding a link
  POST /threads/ID/approvals/APPROVAL    {decision: "allow"|"deny", nonce}   answers that one request, once
  POST /threads/ID/stop                  stops the task and drops what was waiting its turn; what was said stays
  POST /files?name=NAME   (the bytes)    a file from the phone, kept in ~/Downloads/From Fold; {path, bytes}
  GET  /projects                         the project registry (~/ClaudeSync/projects/_projects.json): {name, folders, handoff}
  GET  /projects/NAME/handoff            that project's HANDOFF.md or CURRENT-STATE.md, top section and Next section: {file, updated, text}

Agent mail for a thread (tailshare's ~/.local/share/tailshare/mail/new/, to.session = the thread's id) is taken and
given to that thread labeled as another agent's, never as the owner's words. Each thread's agent runs with
OMA_THREAD_ID set to its thread's id, which `tmsg` signs its mail with, so an answer comes back to the thread.

  oma-runner serve | install | start | stop | status | devices | pair DEVICE | unpair DEVICE | rekey | uninstall

pair gives DEVICE (its Tailscale machine name) a key of its own, sent to it by Taildrop: the phone as mac-runner.json, as
before, and a computer running Soma as soma-runner-<this machine>.json. unpair takes it away at once. rekey is pair
for the phone.
"""
